            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Contributions to Theoretical Physics</strong><br>\nAbdus Salam made numerous contributions to theoretical physics. He is perhaps best known for his role in the development of electroweak theory. This theory explains how electromagnetic force and weak nuclear force are related and can be unified. For this groundbreaking work, he shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1979 with Sheldon Glashow and Steven Weinberg.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Efforts in Science Diplomacy</strong><br>\nBesides his research, Salam was also active in science diplomacy. He strongly believed in the power of science to bridge the divide between developing and developed countries. He was instrumental in founding the International Centre for Theoretical Physics (ICTP) in Trieste, Italy. The ICTP serves as a hub for researchers from around the world to collaborate and learn from each other.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Nobel Prize and Recognition</strong><br>\nIn 1979, Abdus Salam's work on the electroweak theory was honored with the Nobel Prize in Physics. He became the first Pakistani and the first Muslim to receive a Nobel Prize in science. This recognition brought him immense pride but also highlighted his dedication to scientific advancement in the Muslim world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
